  1E31'   00                    	DB	0
                                
                                ;============================================================
                                ; SEARCH  (/ ? n N)
                                ;
                                ; vi's search on WordMaster's matcher.  What it is NOT: vim searches with a
                                ; REGULAR EXPRESSION and this searches for the literal string typed.  An 8080
                                ; has no room for a regex engine; COMMANDS.md records the deviation.  (WM's own
                                ; search wildcards -- ^A any, ^S separator, ^O not -- still reach WCMATCH from
                                ; here, because MATCHF/MATCHB compare through it, but they are WordMaster's
                                ; syntax, not vim's, and nothing advertises them.)
                                ;
                                ; Recorded from vim before any of this was written (VIM_SRCH, accept_vi.py):
                                ;   - '/' starts one character PAST the cursor and lands ON the match's first
                                ;     character; '?' finds the last match that ends before the cursor.
                                ;   - 'wrapscan' is on: a search that runs off one end of the file starts
                                ;     again at the other, so '/' on the file's ONLY match finds it again.
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-94


                                ;   - 'n' repeats the last search's DIRECTION and 'N' reverses it, so 'n'
                                ;     after a '?' keeps going backward.
                                ;   - An empty pattern ('/' then <CR>) repeats the last pattern.
                                ;   - A count repeats the search: '2/a' is the second match, '15n' fifteen
                                ;     n's.  Not found leaves the cursor exactly where it was, at any count.
                                ;   - The window is placed as 'G' places it -- least scroll when the match is
                                ;     near, centred when it is far -- which is why this ends in GPLACE.
                                ;
                                ; This replaces a byte-at-a-time search that ran through CHARAT over logical
                                ; offsets: it could only see the resident window (so a match further into a
                                ; paged file did not exist), had no '?' or 'N', and failed silently.
                                ;============================================================
                                
                                ;============================================================
                                ; SUBSTITUTE  (:s  :%s  :N,Ms  and the 'g' flag)
                                ;
                                ; vi's substitute on WordMaster's matcher, so it finds the same LITERAL string
                                ; the search does -- not a regular expression.  COMMANDS.md records that
                                ; deviation for '/' and it is the same one here, which is why nothing below
                                ; honours '&', '\1' or '~'.  The pattern goes into the SEARCH's own buffer
                                ; because vim's ':s' also sets the last search pattern, so 'n' afterwards
                                ; repeats it.
                                ;
                                ; Recorded from vim before any of this was written (VIM_SUBST, accept_vi.py):
                                ;   - ':s' changes the FIRST match on the cursor's line, ':s...g' every match,
                                ;     and the scan goes on PAST the replacement, so a replacement that
                                ;     contains the pattern is not substituted again.
                                ;   - the cursor lands on the first non-blank of the LAST line changed, placed
                                ;     exactly as 'G' places a line -- which is what GZONE before and GPLACE
                                ;     after are for, and it is measured: ':12800,12800s' leaves the last line
                                ;     on row 22 (top 012778) and ':100,105s' leaves line 105 on row 11.
                                ;   - a pattern that is nowhere in the range changes nothing and moves nothing.
                                ;
                                ; A whole substitute is ONE change for 'u': the second UNDBEG, at the first
                                ; match, is what makes the undo's cursor the first match (where vim leaves it)
                                ; rather than the line the ':' was typed on.  A substitute of more than about a
                                ; dozen lines overruns the undo region, which is accepted -- but it SAYS so,
                                ; both when it happens and when 'u' is then pressed.
                                ;============================================================
                                
                                ;EXADDR -- the optional line range before an ex command: '%' (the whole file),
                                ;'N', or 'N,M'.  SRNGF = 0 when there is none, and SRNG2 = 0FFFFH is the last
                                ;line, whatever it turns out to be.  HL comes back on the command letter.
                                ;
                                ; It does NOT consume the blanks after itself: VC_EX's SKIPB does that, so
                                ; ':2,4 s/a/Z/' reads as ':2,4s/a/Z/' the way ex has always taken it.  The one
                                ; place a blank is still refused is INSIDE the range, around the comma
                                ; (':2, 4s'), which ex accepts and this does not -- the comma is read here off
                                ; DE mid-number and skipping there costs more than the position is worth.
  1E32'                         EXADDR:
  1E32'   AF                    	XRA	A
  1E33'   32 2480'              	STA	SRNGF
  1E36'   7E                    	MOV	A,M
  1E37'   FE 25                 	CPI	'%'
  1E39'   CA 1E62'              	JZ	EXA_AL
  1E3C'   EB                    	XCHG			; DE walks the line, HL takes the number
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-95


  1E3D'   CD 1E77'              	CALL	EXNUM
  1E40'   DA 1E60'              	JC	EXA_NO
  1E43'   22 2481'              	SHLD	SRNG1
  1E46'   22 2483'              	SHLD	SRNG2		; 'N' alone is the single line 'N,N'
  1E49'   1A                    	LDAX	D
  1E4A'   FE 2C                 	CPI	','
  1E4C'   C2 1E59'              	JNZ	EXA_YS
  1E4F'   13                    	INX	D
  1E50'   CD 1E77'              	CALL	EXNUM
  1E53'   DA 1E60'              	JC	EXA_NO		; ',' with no number: not a range at all
  1E56'   22 2483'              	SHLD	SRNG2
  1E59'                         EXA_YS:
  1E59'   EB                    	XCHG
  1E5A'   3E 01                 	MVI	A,1
  1E5C'   32 2480'              	STA	SRNGF
  1E5F'   C9                    	RET
  1E60'                         EXA_NO:
  1E60'   EB                    	XCHG
  1E61'   C9                    	RET
  1E62'                         EXA_AL:
  1E62'   23                    	INX	H
  1E63'   E5                    	PUSH	H
  1E64'   21 0001               	LXI	H,1
  1E67'   22 2481'              	SHLD	SRNG1
  1E6A'   21 FFFF               	LXI	H,0FFFFH
  1E6D'   22 2483'              	SHLD	SRNG2
  1E70'   E1                    	POP	H
  1E71'   3E 01                 	MVI	A,1
  1E73'   32 2480'              	STA	SRNGF
  1E76'   C9                    	RET
                                
                                ;EXNUM -- a decimal number at DE -> HL, DE left past it.  CY when there is no
                                ;digit there at all.
  1E77'                         EXNUM:
  1E77'   1A                    	LDAX	D
  1E78'   D6 30                 	SUI	'0'
  1E7A'   D8                    	RC
  1E7B'   FE 0A                 	CPI	10
  1E7D'   3F                    	CMC
  1E7E'   D8                    	RC
  1E7F'   21 0000               	LXI	H,0
  1E82'                         EXN_LP:
  1E82'   1A                    	LDAX	D
  1E83'   D6 30                 	SUI	'0'
  1E85'   DA 1E9F'              	JC	EXN_DN
  1E88'   FE 0A                 	CPI	10
  1E8A'   D2 1E9F'              	JNC	EXN_DN
  1E8D'   13                    	INX	D
  1E8E'   C5                    	PUSH	B
  1E8F'   4F                    	MOV	C,A
  1E90'   06 00                 	MVI	B,0		; BC = the digit
  1E92'   D5                    	PUSH	D
  1E93'   54                    	MOV	D,H
  1E94'   5D                    	MOV	E,L
  1E95'   29                    	DAD	H		; *2
  1E96'   29                    	DAD	H		; *4
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-96


  1E97'   19                    	DAD	D		; *5
  1E98'   29                    	DAD	H		; *10
  1E99'   D1                    	POP	D
  1E9A'   09                    	DAD	B
  1E9B'   C1                    	POP	B
  1E9C'   C3 1E82'              	JMP	EXN_LP
  1E9F'                         EXN_DN:
  1E9F'   B7                    	ORA	A		; CY clear: there was a number
  1EA0'   C9                    	RET
                                
                                ;EX_S -- ':s': the delimiter, the pattern, the replacement, an optional 'g'.
  1EA1'                         EX_S:
  1EA1'   7E                    	MOV	A,M
  1EA2'   B7                    	ORA	A
  1EA3'   CA 1EE5'              	JZ	SUBNOP		; ':s' alone -- repeating the last one is not
                                				;   built (COMMANDS.md)
  1EA6'   32 2485'              	STA	SDELIM
  1EA9'   23                    	INX	H
  1EAA'   11 0000*              	LXI	D,SRCHST
  1EAD'   3A 0000*              	LDA	SRCMAX		; the pattern's capacity is BUF.MAC's to state
  1EB0'   4F                    	MOV	C,A
  1EB1'   CD 1EEB'              	CALL	SCOPY
  1EB4'   78                    	MOV	A,B
  1EB5'   B7                    	ORA	A
  1EB6'   CA 1EE5'              	JZ	SUBNOP		; ':s//x/' -- an empty pattern matches nothing
  1EB9'   32 0000*              	STA	SRCHBF		; WM's RDSTR layout: the length below the string
  1EBC'   3E 01                 	MVI	A,1
  1EBE'   32 2458'              	STA	SHAVE		; 'n' repeats it, as after a '/'
  1EC1'   11 2492'              	LXI	D,SREPL
  1EC4'   0E 24                 	MVI	C,SREPMX
  1EC6'   CD 1EEB'              	CALL	SCOPY
  1EC9'   78                    	MOV	A,B
  1ECA'   32 2491'              	STA	SREPLN
  1ECD'   AF                    	XRA	A
  1ECE'   32 2486'              	STA	SGFLAG
  1ED1'   7E                    	MOV	A,M
  1ED2'   FE 67                 	CPI	'g'
  1ED4'   C2 1EDD'              	JNZ	EX_SG
  1ED7'   23                    	INX	H
  1ED8'   3E 01                 	MVI	A,1
  1EDA'   32 2486'              	STA	SGFLAG
  1EDD'                         EX_SG:
  1EDD'   7E                    	MOV	A,M
  1EDE'   B7                    	ORA	A
  1EDF'   C2 1CD3'              	JNZ	EX_TRL		; 'Trailing chars: ' -- vim's, shortened
  1EE2'   C3 1F0B'              	JMP	SUBGO
  1EE5'                         SUBNOP:
  1EE5'   21 1D7F'              	LXI	H,SNOPAT
  1EE8'   C3 1CE1'              	JMP	EXERR
                                
                                ;SCOPY -- the text at HL up to SDELIM (or the line's end) into [DE], C its
                                ;capacity: B = how many were taken, HL past the delimiter, and [DE] is left
                                ;0-terminated as WM's RDSTR leaves a string.
  1EEB'                         SCOPY:
  1EEB'   06 00                 	MVI	B,0
  1EED'                         SCP_LP:
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-97


  1EED'   78                    	MOV	A,B
  1EEE'   B9                    	CMP	C
  1EEF'   D2 1F08'              	JNC	SCP_DN		; full: what is left stays on the line, and so
                                				;   comes back as trailing characters
  1EF2'   7E                    	MOV	A,M
  1EF3'   B7                    	ORA	A
  1EF4'   CA 1F08'              	JZ	SCP_DN		; the line ended: no closing delimiter wanted
  1EF7'   E5                    	PUSH	H
  1EF8'   21 2485'              	LXI	H,SDELIM
  1EFB'   BE                    	CMP	M
  1EFC'   E1                    	POP	H
  1EFD'   CA 1F07'              	JZ	SCP_SK
  1F00'   12                    	STAX	D
  1F01'   13                    	INX	D
  1F02'   23                    	INX	H
  1F03'   04                    	INR	B
  1F04'   C3 1EED'              	JMP	SCP_LP
  1F07'                         SCP_SK:
  1F07'   23                    	INX	H
  1F08'                         SCP_DN:
  1F08'   AF                    	XRA	A
  1F09'   12                    	STAX	D
  1F0A'   C9                    	RET
                                
                                ;SUBGO -- run the substitute.  GZONE comes first, before anything moves: the
                                ;landing is placed as 'G' places a line, and GPLACE measures it against the
                                ;screen's top line as it was when the command was typed.
  1F0B'                         SUBGO:
  1F0B'   CD 0DE4'              	CALL	GZONE
  1F0E'   2A 0000*              	LHLD	LNDLT
  1F11'   22 248F'              	SHLD	SLDLTS		; the walk moves the cursor; a substitute that
  1F14'   CD 0000*              	CALL	CUROFF		;   finds nothing puts both back, as the
  1F17'   22 248D'              	SHLD	SOFFS		;   search's SN_BAD does
  1F1A'   AF                    	XRA	A
  1F1B'   32 2487'              	STA	SANY
  1F1E'   21 0000               	LXI	H,0
  1F21'   22 2489'              	SHLD	SBACK
  1F24'   3A 2480'              	LDA	SRNGF
  1F27'   B7                    	ORA	A
  1F28'   CA 1F51'              	JZ	SUB_1L
                                	;--- ':N,Ms' / ':%s': to the range's first line, then walk it ---
  1F2B'   2A 2481'              	LHLD	SRNG1
  1F2E'   7C                    	MOV	A,H
  1F2F'   B5                    	ORA	L
  1F30'   CA 1FCD'              	JZ	SUBBAD		; ':0s' -- there is no line 0
  1F33'   2A 2483'              	LHLD	SRNG2
  1F36'   EB                    	XCHG
  1F37'   2A 2481'              	LHLD	SRNG1
  1F3A'   CD 0000*              	CALL	CMP16		; CY iff SRNG2 < SRNG1
  1F3D'   DA 1FCD'              	JC	SUBBAD		; backwards: vim offers to swap, this refuses
  1F40'   EB                    	XCHG			; HL = the last line, DE = the first
  1F41'   CD 0000*              	CALL	SUBHD
  1F44'   23                    	INX	H		; HL = the lines in the range
  1F45'   22 248B'              	SHLD	SLINES
  1F48'   2A 2481'              	LHLD	SRNG1
  1F4B'   CD 0D72'              	CALL	GOLN
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-98


  1F4E'   C3 1F57'              	JMP	SUBLP
  1F51'                         SUB_1L:
  1F51'   21 0001               	LXI	H,1
  1F54'   22 248B'              	SHLD	SLINES
  1F57'                         SUBLP:
  1F57'   CD 1FD3'              	CALL	SUBLIN		; A = 1 when this line changed
  1F5A'   B7                    	ORA	A
  1F5B'   CA 1F69'              	JZ	SUB_NX
  1F5E'   3E 01                 	MVI	A,1
  1F60'   32 2487'              	STA	SANY
  1F63'   21 0000               	LXI	H,0
  1F66'   22 2489'              	SHLD	SBACK		; the cursor is ON the last line changed
  1F69'                         SUB_NX:
  1F69'   2A 248B'              	LHLD	SLINES
  1F6C'   2B                    	DCX	H
  1F6D'   22 248B'              	SHLD	SLINES
  1F70'   7C                    	MOV	A,H
  1F71'   B5                    	ORA	L
  1F72'   CA 1F90'              	JZ	SUBDON		; the range is spent
  1F75'   21 0001               	LXI	H,1
  1F78'   AF                    	XRA	A
  1F79'   CD 0000*              	CALL	MVLIND		; the next line, paging with it
  1F7C'   3A 2487'              	LDA	SANY
  1F7F'   B7                    	ORA	A
  1F80'   CA 1F8A'              	JZ	SUB_AE		; nothing changed yet: nothing to count from
  1F83'   2A 2489'              	LHLD	SBACK
  1F86'   23                    	INX	H
  1F87'   22 2489'              	SHLD	SBACK		; every step taken after the last change has
                                				;   to be walked back at the end -- including
                                				;   this one, when it lands past the last line
  1F8A'                         SUB_AE:
  1F8A'   CD 08B7'              	CALL	ATEOF
  1F8D'   D2 1F57'              	JNC	SUBLP		; ... unless that ran off the end of the file
  1F90'                         SUBDON:
  1F90'   CD 0000*              	CALL	CLRCNT		; the walk left the count and direction set
  1F93'   3A 2487'              	LDA	SANY
  1F96'   B7                    	ORA	A
  1F97'   CA 1FB5'              	JZ	SUBNFD
  1F9A'   3E 01                 	MVI	A,1
  1F9C'   32 23E5'              	STA	MODF		; the text changed, so ':w' has work to do.  NOT
                                				;   MARKMOD: that also sets DOTCHG, and '.' does
                                				;   not repeat a substitute in vim either (that
                                				;   is '&', which is not built)
  1F9F'   2A 2489'              	LHLD	SBACK
  1FA2'   7C                    	MOV	A,H
  1FA3'   B5                    	ORA	L
  1FA4'   CA 1FAF'              	JZ	SUB_PL
  1FA7'   3E FF                 	MVI	A,0FFH
  1FA9'   CD 0000*              	CALL	MVLIND		; back to the last line changed
  1FAC'   CD 0000*              	CALL	CLRCNT
  1FAF'                         SUB_PL:
  1FAF'   CD 0DFC'              	CALL	GPLACE		; the landing: first non-blank, placed as 'G'
  1FB2'   C3 062E'              	JMP	SETFUL		; GPLACE asks for R_MOVE when the target is
                                				;   which rewrote the line under the cursor.
                                				; And that repaint is why a substitute too big
                                				;   for the undo does NOT say so here: EXMSG
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-99


                                				;   draws the bottom row there and then, and
                                				;   the full paint this needs takes the status
                                				;   row back (SCRN.MAC says so itself).  'u'
                                				;   carries the message instead -- UNDWHY = 2,
                                				;   at the moment the reader asks for the undo
                                				;   and can still act on the answer.
  1FB5'                         SUBNFD:
  1FB5'   2A 248D'              	LHLD	SOFFS
  1FB8'   CD 0000*              	CALL	GOTO
  1FBB'   2A 248F'              	LHLD	SLDLTS
  1FBE'   22 0000*              	SHLD	LNDLT		; nothing changed: the row the cursor was on
  1FC1'   21 1D6B'              	LXI	H,SNOTFD
  1FC4'   CD 1CE1'              	CALL	EXERR
  1FC7'   21 0000*              	LXI	H,SRCHST
  1FCA'   C3 1CDC'              	JMP	EXOUT		; 'Pattern not found: ' and the pattern
  1FCD'                         SUBBAD:
  1FCD'   21 1D59'              	LXI	H,SNOT
  1FD0'   C3 1CD6'              	JMP	EXERT
                                
                                ;SUBLIN -- the substitute on the cursor's line: the first match, or every match
                                ;when 'g' was given.  A = 1 when the line changed.  The cursor is left at the
                                ;line's start either way, so the walk to the next line always starts from one.
                                ;Every offset is re-derived from the cursor after each change, never held
                                ;across one: an insert can page (':%s/0/ZZ/' grows a 100 K file by 12800
                                ;bytes) and paging shifts every logical offset in the window.
  1FD3'                         SUBLIN:
  1FD3'   AF                    	XRA	A
  1FD4'   32 2488'              	STA	SLCHG
  1FD7'   CD 2052'              	CALL	SUBBOL
  1FDA'   CD 0000*              	CALL	RGET0		; the line has to be RESIDENT before CEND can
  1FDD'                         SUBL_R:				;   find its end: the pager keeps the CURSOR in
  1FDD'   CD 0000*              	CALL	RGET		;   the window, not the whole line it sits on,
  1FE0'   DA 1FE8'              	JC	SUBL_L		;   and EOLNL only ever scans resident text --
  1FE3'   FE 0A                 	CPI	LF		;   so a line straddling the resident end came
  1FE5'   C2 1FDD'              	JNZ	SUBL_R		;   out short and went unsubstituted (line 179
                                				;   of the 66 K indented file, which starts at
                                				;   byte 2047).  RGET pages through NEEDIN, as
                                				;   every other line reader here does.
  1FE8'                         SUBL_L:
                                	;--- the scan window: the cursor to the line's content end ---
  1FE8'   CD 0000*              	CALL	CUROFF
  1FEB'   E5                    	PUSH	H
  1FEC'   CD 119D'              	CALL	CEND
  1FEF'   D1                    	POP	D
  1FF0'   CD 0000*              	CALL	SUBHD		; HL = the bytes left on the line
  1FF3'   23                    	INX	H		; FWDCNT's own arithmetic, inline: the span it
  1FF4'   CD 0000*              	CALL	SUBLEN		;   would measure, plus one, less the pattern.
  1FF7'   7C                    	MOV	A,H		;   (FWDCNT needs a real pointer pair and this
  1FF8'   B7                    	ORA	A		;   span is a line, not a pair.)
  1FF9'   FA 204B'              	JM	SUBL_D		; what is left is shorter than the pattern
  1FFC'   44                    	MOV	B,H
  1FFD'   4D                    	MOV	C,L		; BC = the start positions a match can take
  1FFE'   11 0000*              	LXI	D,SRCHST
  2001'   2A 0000*              	LHLD	GAPEND		; with the gap at the cursor, the line runs
  2004'   CD 0000*              	CALL	MATCHF		;   forward from GAPEND
  2007'   DA 204B'              	JC	SUBL_D
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-100


  200A'   CD 0000*              	CALL	SUBLEN		; MATCHF ends past the match; back to its start
  200D'   EB                    	XCHG
  200E'   2A 0000*              	LHLD	GAPEND
  2011'   EB                    	XCHG
  2012'   CD 0000*              	CALL	SUBHD		; HL = how far into the scan the match is
  2015'   EB                    	XCHG
  2016'   D5                    	PUSH	D		; CUROFF does NOT keep DE (it reads TXTBEG into
  2017'   CD 0000*              	CALL	CUROFF		;   it), so the delta goes on the stack
  201A'   D1                    	POP	D
  201B'   19                    	DAD	D		; ... and so the match's logical offset
  201C'   CD 0000*              	CALL	GOTO
                                	;--- the first change of the whole substitute re-arms the undo, so 'u'
                                	;    lands on the first match, which is where vim leaves it ---
  201F'   3A 2487'              	LDA	SANY
  2022'   B7                    	ORA	A
  2023'   C2 2030'              	JNZ	SUBL_C
  2026'   3A 2488'              	LDA	SLCHG
  2029'   B7                    	ORA	A
  202A'   C2 2030'              	JNZ	SUBL_C
  202D'   CD 0000*              	CALL	UNDHER		; the cursor snapshot only: UNDBEG would re-run
                                				;   UNDGRW mid-command, and growing the region
                                				;   where a primitive can see it corrupts the
                                				;   gap ('COPY ERR')
  2030'                         SUBL_C:
  2030'   3A 0000*              	LDA	SRCHBF
  2033'   6F                    	MOV	L,A
  2034'   26 00                 	MVI	H,0
  2036'   CD 0000*              	CALL	DELFWD		; the match goes
  2039'   11 2491'              	LXI	D,SREPLN	; ... and the replacement takes its place, the
  203C'   CD 0000*              	CALL	INSSTR		;   way WM splices one: the length byte with
                                				;   its string above it, 'u' capture included
  203F'                         SUBL_I:
  203F'   3E 01                 	MVI	A,1
  2041'   32 2488'              	STA	SLCHG
  2044'   3A 2486'              	LDA	SGFLAG
  2047'   B7                    	ORA	A
  2048'   C2 1FE8'              	JNZ	SUBL_L		; 'g': on from PAST the replacement
  204B'                         SUBL_D:
  204B'   CD 2052'              	CALL	SUBBOL
  204E'   3A 2488'              	LDA	SLCHG
  2051'   C9                    	RET
                                
                                ;SUBBOL -- the cursor to the start of the line it is on.
  2052'                         SUBBOL:
  2052'   CD 0000*              	CALL	CUROFF
  2055'   CD 04F9'              	CALL	BOL
  2058'   C3 0000*              	JMP	GOTO
                                
                                ;VC_SRCH -- '/' and '?': read the pattern, then search.  C is the key, which
                                ; is both the prompt character and the direction.
  205B'                         VC_SRCH:
  205B'   CD 060D'              	CALL	SETMOV
  205E'   C5                    	PUSH	B
  205F'   CD 05A0'              	CALL	GETCNT
  2062'   22 2479'              	SHLD	SCNT
  2065'   C1                    	POP	B
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-101


  2066'   79                    	MOV	A,C
  2067'   FE 2F                 	CPI	'/'
  2069'   3E 00                 	MVI	A,0
  206B'   CA 2070'              	JZ	VS_DIR
  206E'   3E FF                 	MVI	A,0FFH		; '?' -- backward
  2070'                         VS_DIR:
  2070'   32 2456'              	STA	SDIR
  2073'   11 0000*              	LXI	D,SRCHST	; the line is read straight into the pattern
  2076'   3A 0000*              	LDA	SRCMAX		; its capacity is BUF.MAC's to state
  2079'   47                    	MOV	B,A
  207A'   CD 1831'              	CALL	RDLINE
  207D'   B7                    	ORA	A
  207E'   C0                    	RNZ			; cancelled: nothing happens at all
  207F'   3A 242E'              	LDA	RLLEN
  2082'   B7                    	ORA	A
  2083'   CA 2098'              	JZ	VS_OLD		; an empty line repeats the last pattern --
                                				;   RDLINE stored nothing, so it is still there
  2086'   5F                    	MOV	E,A
  2087'   16 00                 	MVI	D,0
  2089'   21 0000*              	LXI	H,SRCHST
  208C'   19                    	DAD	D
  208D'   36 00                 	MVI	M,0		; 0-terminated, and the length below it:
  208F'   7B                    	MOV	A,E
  2090'   32 0000*              	STA	SRCHBF		;   WM's RDSTR layout, built by hand
  2093'   3E 01                 	MVI	A,1
  2095'   32 2458'              	STA	SHAVE
  2098'                         VS_OLD:
  2098'   3A 2458'              	LDA	SHAVE
  209B'   B7                    	ORA	A
  209C'   CA 20A8'              	JZ	VS_NOP		; '/' <CR> with nothing ever searched for
  209F'   3A 2456'              	LDA	SDIR
  20A2'   32 2457'              	STA	SDIRE
  20A5'   C3 20D1'              	JMP	SRCHN
  20A8'                         VS_NOP:
  20A8'   21 1D7F'              	LXI	H,SNOPAT
  20AB'   C3 1CE1'              	JMP	EXERR
                                
                                ;VC_N / VCB_N -- 'n' repeats the last search, 'N' reverses its direction.
  20AE'                         VC_N:
  20AE'   CD 060D'              	CALL	SETMOV
  20B1'   3A 2456'              	LDA	SDIR
  20B4'   C3 20BE'              	JMP	VN_GO
  20B7'                         VCB_N:
  20B7'   CD 060D'              	CALL	SETMOV
  20BA'   3A 2456'              	LDA	SDIR
  20BD'   2F                    	CMA			; the other way: 0 <-> FFH
  20BE'                         VN_GO:
  20BE'   32 2457'              	STA	SDIRE
  20C1'   CD 05A0'              	CALL	GETCNT
  20C4'   22 2479'              	SHLD	SCNT
  20C7'   3A 2458'              	LDA	SHAVE
  20CA'   B7                    	ORA	A
  20CB'   CA 20A8'              	JZ	VS_NOP
  20CE'   C3 20D1'              	JMP	SRCHN
                                
                                ;SRCHN -- the count, one whole-file search each.  Not found at ANY repeat
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-102


                                ; leaves the cursor where the command started, as vim does, and says so; the
                                ; message wants the screen left alone, which is what EXERR's R_MOVE is for.
  20D1'                         SRCHN:
  20D1'   CD 0DE4'              	CALL	GZONE		; the screen's top line, before the cursor moves
  20D4'   2A 0000*              	LHLD	LNDLT
  20D7'   22 247D'              	SHLD	SLDLT		; the sweep moves the cursor with PUTCUR, which
                                				;   does not maintain LNDLT (GOTO does)
  20DA'   CD 0000*              	CALL	CUROFF
  20DD'   22 247B'              	SHLD	SOFF
  20E0'                         SN_LP:
  20E0'   CD 2118'              	CALL	SRCHGO
  20E3'   DA 20FD'              	JC	SN_BAD
  20E6'   2A 2479'              	LHLD	SCNT
  20E9'   2B                    	DCX	H
  20EA'   22 2479'              	SHLD	SCNT
  20ED'   7C                    	MOV	A,H
  20EE'   B5                    	ORA	L
  20EF'   C2 20E0'              	JNZ	SN_LP
  20F2'   CD 0000*              	CALL	CLRCNT		; the sweep left CMDCNT wide open (and DIRFLG
                                				;   backward): WM clears both per command
  20F5'   3E 01                 	MVI	A,1
  20F7'   32 2463'              	STA	GPKEEP		; land on the match, not the first non-blank
  20FA'   C3 0DFC'              	JMP	GPLACE		; (which sets LNDLT itself)
  20FD'                         SN_BAD:
  20FD'   CD 0000*              	CALL	CLRCNT
  2100'   2A 247B'              	LHLD	SOFF
  2103'   CD 0000*              	CALL	GOTO
  2106'   2A 247D'              	LHLD	SLDLT
  2109'   22 0000*              	SHLD	LNDLT		; nothing moved: the row the cursor was on
  210C'   21 1D6B'              	LXI	H,SNOTFD
  210F'   CD 1CE1'              	CALL	EXERR
  2112'   21 0000*              	LXI	H,SRCHST
  2115'   C3 1CDC'              	JMP	EXOUT		; 'Pattern not found: ' and the pattern
                                
                                ;SRCHGO -- one search of the whole file, wrapping once: from the cursor to the
                                ; end in SDIRE's direction, then from the other end back to it.  CY = the
                                ; pattern is nowhere in the file (and the cursor is left at an end of it --
                                ; SRCHN is what puts it back).
  2118'                         SRCHGO:
  2118'   3E 01                 	MVI	A,1
  211A'   32 247F'              	STA	SSKIP		; this pass starts one past the cursor
  211D'   CD 212F'              	CALL	SRCHW
  2120'   D0                    	RNC
  2121'   3A 2457'              	LDA	SDIRE
  2124'   2F                    	CMA
  2125'   CD 0000*              	CALL	MVLMAX		; to the end the wrapped pass starts from
  2128'   AF                    	XRA	A
  2129'   32 247F'              	STA	SSKIP		; ... but the wrapped pass starts AT it, or a
                                				;   match on the file's very first character
                                				;   would be stepped over and never found
  212C'   C3 212F'              	JMP	SRCHW
                                
                                ;SRCHW -- WM FINDA (WM 1943): the file from the cursor on, one resident
                                ; window at a time.  A miss moves the cursor to that window's edge and pages
                                ; one step (SCRLST), so the search runs off the window and through the file;
                                ; CY when the file runs out.  PAGESTP only pages while the count is unspent,
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-103


                                ; so the sweep opens CMDCNT wide, as MVLMAX does.  WM's FINDA then puts the
                                ; cursor exactly at the file's end (WM 1949); nothing here needs that -- the
                                ; caller either wraps to the other end or restores the cursor outright.
  212F'                         SRCHW:
  212F'   3A 2457'              	LDA	SDIRE
  2132'   32 0000*              	STA	DIRFLG
  2135'   21 FFFF               	LXI	H,0FFFFH
  2138'   22 23E2'              	SHLD	CMDCNT
  213B'   AF                    	XRA	A
  213C'   32 0000*              	STA	AUXCNT
  213F'                         SW_LP:
  213F'   CD 214B'              	CALL	SRCH1
  2142'   D0                    	RNC
  2143'   CD 0000*              	CALL	SCRLST
  2146'   D2 213F'              	JNC	SW_LP
  2149'   37                    	STC
  214A'   C9                    	RET
                                
                                ;SRCH1 -- WM FINDB (WM 1984) without its RDSTR: one search inside the
                                ; resident window, DIRFLG picking the direction.  CY = not in this window; on
                                ; a hit the cursor is ON the match's first character.
  214B'                         SRCH1:
  214B'   11 0000*              	LXI	D,SRCHST
  214E'   3A 0000*              	LDA	DIRFLG
  2151'   B7                    	ORA	A
  2152'   FA 2176'              	JM	SR1_BK
  2155'   21 0000*              	LXI	H,GAPEND
  2158'   CD 0000*              	CALL	FWDCNT
  215B'   2A 0000*              	LHLD	GAPEND		; WM loads the pointer BEFORE testing the
  215E'   D8                    	RC			;   carry: a miss still has to leave HL a
                                				;   real text address, because SCRLST puts
                                				;   the cursor there on the way to paging
  215F'   3A 247F'              	LDA	SSKIP
  2162'   B7                    	ORA	A
  2163'   CA 216C'              	JZ	SR1_FW
  2166'   78                    	MOV	A,B
  2167'   B1                    	ORA	C
  2168'   37                    	STC
  2169'   C8                    	RZ			; no room for a match even before the skip
  216A'   0B                    	DCX	B		; vim's '/' starts one character PAST the
  216B'   23                    	INX	H		;   cursor, so the character under it can
                                				;   never be the one that matches
  216C'                         SR1_FW:
  216C'   CD 0000*              	CALL	MATCHF
  216F'   D8                    	RC
  2170'   CD 0000*              	CALL	SUBLEN		; MATCHF ends PAST the match; vi lands on it
  2173'   C3 0000*              	JMP	PUTCUR
  2176'                         SR1_BK:
  2176'   21 0000*              	LXI	H,TXTBEG
  2179'   CD 0000*              	CALL	FWDCNT
  217C'   2A 0000*              	LHLD	GAPBEG		; (as above: the pointer, then the carry)
  217F'   D8                    	RC
  2180'   CD 0000*              	CALL	SUBLEN		; MATCHB already lands on the first character
  2183'   CD 0000*              	CALL	MATCHB
  2186'   D8                    	RC
  2187'   C3 0000*              	JMP	PUTCUR
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-104


                                
                                ;============================================================
                                ; DISPATCH TABLES -- the messages first, then CMDTAB and ACTTAB.
                                ;============================================================
                                ;============================================================
                                ; MESSAGES -- the bottom row is vim's message line: blank unless something has
                                ; been said on it.  A message is built by successive calls -- EXPRM first, which
                                ; clears the row, then the pieces straight to the terminal.  There is no
                                ; concatenation buffer and none is wanted: the pieces are a name, a flag and a
                                ; number, and each knows how to write itself.
                                ;
                                ; Every message is drawn AFTER the frame (MSGPST, from the main loop), because
                                ; a full repaint blanks the bottom row -- so a message written during the
                                ; command would be wiped by the repaint that same key causes.
                                ;============================================================
                                
                                ;MSGNAM -- open the row and write the file name at HL (a 12-byte FCB field:
                                ; drive, 8 name, 3 type) in quotes, the way vim opens a message: "TEST.TXT".
                                ; A blank name field is vim's "[No Name]".  vim writes a path relative to the
                                ; current directory -- a file in it bare, "../x/VI.MAC" otherwise -- and the
                                ; logged drive is CP/M's current directory, so the drive is written only when
                                ; it is another one: "B:TEST.TXT".
  218A'                         MSGNAM:
  218A'   E5                    	PUSH	H
  218B'   3E 22                 	MVI	A,'"'
  218D'   CD 0000*              	CALL	EXPRM		; clears the row, writes the opening quote
  2190'   E1                    	POP	H
  2191'   E5                    	PUSH	H		; keep the base: the type field is 9 past it
  2192'   23                    	INX	H
  2193'   7E                    	MOV	A,M
  2194'   FE 20                 	CPI	' '
  2196'   C2 21A3'              	JNZ	MN_HAS
  2199'   21 1DCE'              	LXI	H,SNONM
  219C'   CD 0000*              	CALL	OUTSTR
  219F'   E1                    	POP	H
  21A0'   C3 21ED'              	JMP	MN_END
  21A3'                         MN_HAS:
  21A3'   0E 19                 	MVI	C,BF_CURDSK
  21A5'   CD 0005               	CALL	BDOS		; A = the logged drive, A: = 0
  21A8'   E1                    	POP	H
  21A9'   E5                    	PUSH	H
  21AA'   3C                    	INR	A		; the FCB's drive byte is explicit, A: = 1
  21AB'   BE                    	CMP	M
  21AC'   CA 21BA'              	JZ	MN_NAM
  21AF'   7E                    	MOV	A,M
  21B0'   C6 40                 	ADI	'A'-1
  21B2'   CD 0000*              	CALL	OUTCH
  21B5'   3E 3A                 	MVI	A,':'
  21B7'   CD 0000*              	CALL	OUTCH
  21BA'                         MN_NAM:
  21BA'   E1                    	POP	H
  21BB'   E5                    	PUSH	H
  21BC'   23                    	INX	H
  21BD'   0E 08                 	MVI	C,8
  21BF'                         MN_NM:
  21BF'   7E                    	MOV	A,M
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-105


  21C0'   FE 20                 	CPI	' '
  21C2'   CA 21CD'              	JZ	MN_EXT
  21C5'   CD 0000*              	CALL	OUTCH
  21C8'   23                    	INX	H
  21C9'   0D                    	DCR	C
  21CA'   C2 21BF'              	JNZ	MN_NM
  21CD'                         MN_EXT:
  21CD'   E1                    	POP	H
  21CE'   11 0009               	LXI	D,9
  21D1'   19                    	DAD	D		; the type field
  21D2'   7E                    	MOV	A,M
  21D3'   FE 20                 	CPI	' '
  21D5'   CA 21ED'              	JZ	MN_END
  21D8'   3E 2E                 	MVI	A,'.'
  21DA'   CD 0000*              	CALL	OUTCH
  21DD'   0E 03                 	MVI	C,3
  21DF'                         MN_XL:
  21DF'   7E                    	MOV	A,M
  21E0'   FE 20                 	CPI	' '
  21E2'   CA 21ED'              	JZ	MN_END
  21E5'   CD 0000*              	CALL	OUTCH
  21E8'   23                    	INX	H
  21E9'   0D                    	DCR	C
  21EA'   C2 21DF'              	JNZ	MN_XL
  21ED'                         MN_END:
  21ED'   3E 22                 	MVI	A,'"'
  21EF'   C3 0000*              	JMP	OUTCH
                                
                                ;MSGFLG -- the flags after the name, in vim's order and with vim's spelling:
                                ; [Modified][readonly][New], run together with ONE space before the first of
                                ; them (measured from vim 9.1).  A = 0 leaves [Modified] out, which is the load
                                ; message, where nothing has been changed yet.
  21F2'                         MSGFLG:
  21F2'   06 00                 	MVI	B,0		; B: a flag has been written (the space goes once)
  21F4'   B7                    	ORA	A
  21F5'   CA 2205'              	JZ	MF_RO
  21F8'   3A 23E5'              	LDA	MODF
  21FB'   B7                    	ORA	A
  21FC'   CA 2205'              	JZ	MF_RO
  21FF'   21 1DD8'              	LXI	H,SMODM
  2202'   CD 221A'              	CALL	MF_PUT
  2205'                         MF_RO:
  2205'   3A 2444'              	LDA	ROFLAG
  2208'   B7                    	ORA	A
  2209'   CA 2212'              	JZ	MF_NEW
  220C'   21 1DE3'              	LXI	H,SROM
  220F'   CD 221A'              	CALL	MF_PUT
  2212'                         MF_NEW:
  2212'   3A 2442'              	LDA	MSGNEW
  2215'   B7                    	ORA	A
  2216'   C8                    	RZ
  2217'   21 1DEE'              	LXI	H,SNEWM
  221A'                         MF_PUT:
  221A'   78                    	MOV	A,B
  221B'   B7                    	ORA	A
  221C'   C2 2228'              	JNZ	MF_STR
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-106


  221F'   06 01                 	MVI	B,1
  2221'   E5                    	PUSH	H
  2222'   3E 20                 	MVI	A,' '
  2224'   CD 0000*              	CALL	OUTCH
  2227'   E1                    	POP	H
  2228'                         MF_STR:
  2228'   C3 0000*              	JMP	OUTSTR
                                
                                ;EMITW -- HL as unsigned decimal, no leading zeros (0 still prints one digit).
                                ; Repeated subtraction of each power of ten, which is all an 8080 has.  B
                                ; carries 'a digit has been printed' so the leading zeros are skipped.
  222B'                         EMITW:
  222B'   C5                    	PUSH	B
  222C'   D5                    	PUSH	D
  222D'   06 00                 	MVI	B,0
  222F'   11 2710               	LXI	D,10000
  2232'   CD 2250'              	CALL	EWDIG
  2235'   11 03E8               	LXI	D,1000
  2238'   CD 2250'              	CALL	EWDIG
  223B'   11 0064               	LXI	D,100
  223E'   CD 2250'              	CALL	EWDIG
  2241'   11 000A               	LXI	D,10
  2244'   CD 2250'              	CALL	EWDIG
  2247'   7D                    	MOV	A,L		; the units digit is always printed
  2248'   C6 30                 	ADI	'0'
  224A'   CD 0000*              	CALL	OUTCH
  224D'   D1                    	POP	D
  224E'   C1                    	POP	B
  224F'   C9                    	RET
  2250'                         EWDIG:
  2250'   0E 00                 	MVI	C,0
  2252'                         EW_LP:
  2252'   7D                    	MOV	A,L
  2253'   93                    	SUB	E
  2254'   6F                    	MOV	L,A
  2255'   7C                    	MOV	A,H
  2256'   9A                    	SBB	D
  2257'   67                    	MOV	H,A
  2258'   DA 225F'              	JC	EW_DN
  225B'   0C                    	INR	C
  225C'   C3 2252'              	JMP	EW_LP
  225F'                         EW_DN:
  225F'   19                    	DAD	D		; one subtraction too many: give it back
  2260'   79                    	MOV	A,C
  2261'   B7                    	ORA	A
  2262'   C2 2268'              	JNZ	EW_PR
  2265'   78                    	MOV	A,B
  2266'   B7                    	ORA	A
  2267'   C8                    	RZ			; a leading zero: write nothing
  2268'                         EW_PR:
  2268'   79                    	MOV	A,C
  2269'   C6 30                 	ADI	'0'
  226B'   CD 0000*              	CALL	OUTCH
  226E'   06 01                 	MVI	B,1
  2270'   C9                    	RET
                                
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-107


                                ;ISEMPT -- Z iff the buffer holds nothing at all: no resident text, and nothing
                                ; spilled behind the window either.  vim's '--No lines in buffer--', shortened.
  2271'                         ISEMPT:
  2271'   2A 0000*              	LHLD	OUTPOS
  2274'   7C                    	MOV	A,H
  2275'   B5                    	ORA	L
  2276'   C0                    	RNZ
  2277'   CD 0000*              	CALL	LOGLEN
  227A'   7C                    	MOV	A,H
  227B'   B5                    	ORA	L
  227C'   C9                    	RET
                                
                                ;MSGCTG -- '^G': the file, its flags, and where the cursor is.  DEVIATION: vim
                                ; also gives the file's line total and a percentage of it; no line total is kept
                                ; anywhere here, and counting one means a sweep of the whole file out to EOF and
                                ; a page back -- which would cost the undo region (PGTOPM) as well as the wait.
  227D'                         MSGCTG:
  227D'   21 0000*              	LXI	H,FNAME
  2280'   CD 218A'              	CALL	MSGNAM
  2283'   3E 01                 	MVI	A,1		; '^G' is where vim shows [Modified]
  2285'   CD 21F2'              	CALL	MSGFLG
  2288'   CD 2271'              	CALL	ISEMPT
  228B'   C2 2294'              	JNZ	MG_POS
  228E'   21 1E01'              	LXI	H,SNOLIN
  2291'   C3 0000*              	JMP	OUTSTR
  2294'                         MG_POS:
  2294'   21 1DF4'              	LXI	H,SLINE
  2297'   CD 0000*              	CALL	OUTSTR
  229A'   CD 0000*              	CALL	CNTLF		; the line feeds before the cursor ...
  229D'   23                    	INX	H		;   ... so the line it is on
  229E'   CD 222B'              	CALL	EMITW
  22A1'   21 1DFB'              	LXI	H,SCOLM
  22A4'   CD 0000*              	CALL	OUTSTR
  22A7'   2A 0000*              	LHLD	CBOL		; the byte column within the line, 0-based
  22AA'   23                    	INX	H
  22AB'   E5                    	PUSH	H
  22AC'   CD 222B'              	CALL	EMITW
  22AF'   2A 0000*              	LHLD	CURDCL		; the column a TAB expands that byte to
  22B2'   23                    	INX	H
  22B3'   D1                    	POP	D
  22B4'   CD 0000*              	CALL	CMP16		; the same -> vim writes one number, not two
  22B7'   C8                    	RZ
  22B8'   E5                    	PUSH	H
  22B9'   3E 2D                 	MVI	A,'-'
  22BB'   CD 0000*              	CALL	OUTCH
  22BE'   E1                    	POP	H
  22BF'   C3 222B'              	JMP	EMITW
                                
                                ;FILMSG -- the load message: the file and its flags.  vim adds the line and
                                ; byte totals ('3L, 30B'); the line total is the sweep MSGCTG's note describes.
                                ; A buffer with no file name gets no message at all, as in vim.
  22C2'                         FILMSG:
  22C2'   3A 0001*              	LDA	FNAME+1
  22C5'   FE 20                 	CPI	' '
  22C7'   C8                    	RZ
  22C8'   21 0000*              	LXI	H,FNAME
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-108


  22CB'   CD 218A'              	CALL	MSGNAM
  22CE'   AF                    	XRA	A		; nothing has been changed yet
  22CF'   C3 21F2'              	JMP	MSGFLG
                                
                                ;WRMSG -- what ':w' says.  vim counts what it wrote ('"TEST.TXT" 3L, 29B
                                ; written'); the counts are the same sweep, so this says only that it happened.
                                ; The name is TFCB's, so ':w OTHER.TXT' names the file it really wrote.
  22D2'                         WRMSG:
  22D2'   21 0000*              	LXI	H,TFCB
  22D5'   CD 218A'              	CALL	MSGNAM
  22D8'   21 1E13'              	LXI	H,SWRIT
  22DB'   C3 0000*              	JMP	OUTSTR
                                
                                ;MSGPST -- draw the message this key asked for, once the frame is painted, and
                                ; put the cursor back.  The main loop calls it after SCDRAW and MODEMS.
  22DE'                         MSGPST:
  22DE'   3A 2441'              	LDA	MSGWHT
  22E1'   B7                    	ORA	A
  22E2'   C8                    	RZ
  22E3'   47                    	MOV	B,A
  22E4'   AF                    	XRA	A
  22E5'   32 2441'              	STA	MSGWHT
  22E8'   78                    	MOV	A,B
  22E9'   FE 01                 	CPI	MW_FIL
  22EB'   CA 22F9'              	JZ	MP_FIL
  22EE'   FE 02                 	CPI	MW_WRT
  22F0'   CA 22FF'              	JZ	MP_WRT
  22F3'   CD 227D'              	CALL	MSGCTG
  22F6'   C3 0000*              	JMP	SCURS
  22F9'                         MP_FIL:
  22F9'   CD 22C2'              	CALL	FILMSG
  22FC'   C3 0000*              	JMP	SCURS
  22FF'                         MP_WRT:
  22FF'   CD 22D2'              	CALL	WRMSG
  2302'   C3 0000*              	JMP	SCURS
                                
                                ;VC_CTLG -- '^G'.  It changes nothing and moves nothing, so the frame is a
                                ; still one and the message goes on after it.
  2305'                         VC_CTLG:
  2305'   3E 03                 	MVI	A,MW_CTG
  2307'   32 2441'              	STA	MSGWHT
  230A'   3E 01                 	MVI	A,R_MOVE
  230C'   32 23E6'              	STA	RINTENT
  230F'   C9                    	RET
                                
  2310'                         CMDTAB:
  2310'   07                    	DB	07H			; ^G -- where am I?
  2311'   2305'                 	DW	VC_CTLG
  2313'   68                    	DB	'h'
  2314'   0666'                 	DW	VC_H
  2316'   6C                    	DB	'l'
  2317'   0690'                 	DW	VC_L
  2319'   6A                    	DB	'j'
  231A'   0755'                 	DW	VC_J
  231C'   6B                    	DB	'k'
  231D'   0764'                 	DW	VC_K
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-109


  231F'   30                    	DB	'0'
  2320'   06B7'                 	DW	VC_0
  2322'   5E                    	DB	'^'
  2323'   06D5'                 	DW	VC_CRT
  2325'   24                    	DB	'$'
  2326'   0713'                 	DW	VC_EOL
  2328'   0D                    	DB	CR			; <CR> -- down to the first non-blank
  2329'   0737'                 	DW	VC_CR
  232B'   77                    	DB	'w'
  232C'   0939'                 	DW	VC_W
  232E'   62                    	DB	'b'
  232F'   098A'                 	DW	VC_B
  2331'   65                    	DB	'e'
  2332'   0AEF'                 	DW	VC_E
  2334'   57                    	DB	'W'
  2335'   0940'                 	DW	VCBGW
  2337'   42                    	DB	'B'
  2338'   0991'                 	DW	VCBGB
  233A'   45                    	DB	'E'
  233B'   0AF6'                 	DW	VCBGE
  233D'   47                    	DB	'G'
  233E'   0D51'                 	DW	VC_G
  2340'   67                    	DB	'g'
  2341'   0D85'                 	DW	VC_LG
  2343'   78                    	DB	'x'
  2344'   1166'                 	DW	VC_X
  2346'   5A                    	DB	'Z'
  2347'   18B1'                 	DW	VC_BZ
  2349'   66                    	DB	'f'
  234A'   023A'                 	DW	VC_FF
  234C'   46                    	DB	'F'
  234D'   023F'                 	DW	VC_FB
  234F'   74                    	DB	't'
  2350'   0244'                 	DW	VC_TF
  2352'   54                    	DB	'T'
  2353'   0249'                 	DW	VC_TB
  2355'   3B                    	DB	';'
  2356'   0265'                 	DW	VC_SEMI
  2358'   2C                    	DB	','
  2359'   026F'                 	DW	VC_COMMA
  235B'   79                    	DB	'y'
  235C'   1435'                 	DW	VC_Y
  235E'   59                    	DB	'Y'
  235F'   143D'                 	DW	VY_YY
  2361'   70                    	DB	'p'
  2362'   1629'                 	DW	VC_P
  2364'   50                    	DB	'P'
  2365'   163E'                 	DW	VCB_P
  2367'   69                    	DB	'i'
  2368'   11BE'                 	DW	VC_I
  236A'   61                    	DB	'a'
  236B'   11C4'                 	DW	VC_A
  236D'   41                    	DB	'A'
